The code statements that lead to the bug are:
spin_lock_bh(&wdev->pmsr_lock);
list_for_each_entry(req, &wdev->pmsr_list, list) {
	found = true;
	req->nl_portid = 0;
}
spin_unlock_bh(&wdev->pmsr_lock);
if (found)
	cfg80211_pmsr_process_abort(wdev);

Reason:
When an nl80211 socket is closed, `cfg80211_release_pmsr()` sets `req->nl_portid = 0` and schedules `pmsr_free_wk` to process the abort asynchronously. If the wireless device is torn down before that work runs, `cfg80211_pmsr_wdev_down()` directly calls `cfg80211_pmsr_process_abort()` without canceling the already-scheduled work item. This leaves a pending work that may execute after the interface has been removed, causing the driver's `abort_pmsr` callback to operate on an invalid interface, leading to undefined behavior or crashes.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
spin_lock_bh(&wdev->pmsr_lock);
list_for_each_entry(req, &wdev->pmsr_list, list) {
	found = true;
	req->nl_portid = 0;
}
spin_unlock_bh(&wdev->pmsr_lock);
cancel_work_sync(&wdev->pmsr_free_wk);
if (found)
	cfg80211_pmsr_process_abort(wdev);

Reason:
Adding `cancel_work_sync(&wdev->pmsr_free_wk)` ensures that any pending or in-progress `pmsr_free_wk` work item is fully drained before the abort processing and subsequent interface teardown occur. This prevents the work from invoking the driver's abort callback after the interface is gone, thereby avoiding undefined behavior and potential crashes.

